In Redis, “manual operations with collections” usually means working directly with Redis data structures like List, Set, Hash, Sorted Set using commands.

For Java interview perspective, these are very important.

1. Redis String

Used for simple key-value.

SET user:name "Ali"
GET user:name
DEL user:name

Java example:

redisTemplate.opsForValue().set("user:name", "Ali");
String name = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get("user:name");

2. Redis List

Works like queue/stack.

Manual Commands
LPUSH fruits "apple"
LPUSH fruits "banana"

RPUSH fruits "mango"

LRANGE fruits 0 -1

Output:

banana
apple
mango

Important Operations
LPOP fruits
RPOP fruits
LLEN fruits

Use Cases
Queue
Recent searches
Notifications

3. Redis Set

Stores unique values.

Manual Commands
SADD users "Ali"
SADD users "Ahmed"
SADD users "Ali"

SMEMBERS users

Ali stored only once.

Operations
SREM users "Ali"
SISMEMBER users "Ahmed"
SCARD users

Use Cases
Unique users
Tags
Permissions

4. Redis Hash

Like Java Map<String, String>.

Manual Commands
HSET employee id 101
HSET employee name "Ali"
HSET employee city "Lahore"

HGET employee name
HGETALL employee

Operations
HDEL employee city
HEXISTS employee name

Use Cases
User profile
Object storage

5. Redis Sorted Set (VERY IMPORTANT)

Stores score + value.

Manual Commands
ZADD leaderboard 100 Ali
ZADD leaderboard 200 Ahmed
ZADD leaderboard 150 Bilal

Get Data
ZRANGE leaderboard 0 -1
ZRANGE leaderboard 0 -1 WITHSCORES

Reverse Order
ZREVRANGE leaderboard 0 -1 WITHSCORES

Use Cases
Leaderboard
Ranking
Priority queue

6. Expiry Operations
SET otp "1234"
EXPIRE otp 60
TTL otp
Auto delete after 60 sec

Used heavily for:

OTP
Session
Cache

7. Redis Collection Operations in Spring Boot
List
redisTemplate.opsForList().leftPush("fruits", "apple");
Set
redisTemplate.opsForSet().add("users", "Ali");
Hash
redisTemplate.opsForHash().put("employee", "name", "Ali");
Sorted Set
redisTemplate.opsForZSet().add("leaderboard", "Ali", 100);

Important Interview Question
Why Redis collections are fast?

Because Redis:

Stores data in memory (RAM)
Uses optimized internal data structures
Single-threaded event loop avoids locking overhead

Common Interview Use Cases
| Collection | Use Case      |
| ---------- | ------------- |
| String     | Cache, OTP    |
| List       | Queue         |
| Set        | Unique values |
| Hash       | User object   |
| Sorted Set | Ranking       |

Difference Between List and Set
| List             | Set                 |
| ---------------- | ------------------- |
| Allows duplicate | Unique only         |
| Ordered          | Unordered           |
| Queue operations | Membership checking |

Difference Between Hash and String
| String                 | Hash               |
| ---------------------- | ------------------ |
| Single value           | Multiple fields    |
| Whole object serialize | Field-wise storage |


Example:

SET user "{name:Ali,age:25}"

vs

HSET user name Ali
HSET user age 25